# Chats — pull requests, stage progress and changes

**Pull requests and changes:**

- A thread's pull requests are only those it opened or its CLI recorded it touching (the run wire's `pullRequests`, each marked `opened`; a task card shows only the opened ones), addressed by URL (`use-thread-pull-requests.ts` over `readPullRequestsByRef`). That hook is the only source for the panel, the sidebar and the shelf.
- A pull request's recorded outcome (`record_outcome`) is the daemon's `DisplayedPullRequest.outcome`, never folded from the window: the shelf chip draws its acceptance mark beside the number from it, and a `run_outcome` transcript row reads superseded once that outcome's seq is newer than its own (`RunOutcomesContext`). The row opens on a press into the agent's summary, every figure and the pull request's link row; its title and state come from the same `use-thread-pull-requests.ts` answers the panel draws (`RunOutcomes.resolved`), so a surface that asks none draws the number alone. `daemon-client.ts` carries it through every run-status announce. The agents panel's Pull requests section draws a pull request's whole outcome history under its row (`chats/pull-request-outcome-history.tsx`, folded per thread to the newest verdict), read from the daemon (`GET /v1/chats/:runId/outcomes`, `use-run-outcomes.ts` over `use-run-daemon-read.ts`, the one whole-run read the artifacts and team-notes lists share) and re-read when the newest outcome seq on the run's list moves.
- A team run's stage progress is the daemon's `RunDto.stages` (`StageProgress`), replaced whole by every `run_status.stages` (`run-status-row.ts`) — never folded from `stage_change` rows, so `n/m` and every status hold past the loaded window. The Progress window's Stages tab (`progress-dialog.tsx`; its stage rows are `stage-entry.tsx`), the agents-panel Stages section (moves through `moveTeamRunStage` with `expectedRev`; a `409 STAGE_PROGRESS_CHANGED` keeps the dialog open) and the transcript row (`stage-change-row.tsx`, next-stage titles from `RunStagesContext`, provided once with the transcript's values (`TranscriptContexts`); a move's row reads superseded once its stage's `StageProgressEntry.rev` names a later move, a nudge's once the run's `rev` moved on) all draw it, and every stage graph is the one `components/stage-graph.tsx`: React Flow nodes laid out and routed by ELK (`stage-graph-layout.ts`, orthogonal wires, so a wire that skips stages runs beside them and never across a pill), drawn once its lazy chunk and the layout land, so a spec reading its pills awaits `__tests__/settle-stage-graphs.ts`. The board card's line reads `TaskDto.stage`; `use-board.ts` reloads on a stages-only announce for a run it holds.
- A sidebar row names the thread's newest resolved pull request among those it opened, and a touched one only for a thread that opened none — never in place of an opened one that did not resolve (`rowPullRequestOf`).
- A ref GitHub could not answer still draws a row.
- The renderer replaces the run's list wholesale on each announce.
- Do not reintroduce a branch-scoped pull-request list.
- `keepFresherRows`: a pin, colour or pull-request list written without moving `updatedAt` is decided by the listing. So are the live readings (`awaiting`, `holdingFor`, the shell/sub-agent/call counts), unless an announce of them arrived while the listing was in flight: no write stamps them, and an announce's `at` always postdates the row's `updatedAt`.
- "Changed since this chat started" uses `upstreamBaseSince` and excludes what a pull brought in. The header names the ref and claims no cause.
- A folder on one of the thread's open pull requests is measured from that pull request's base branch, so a stacked branch lists what GitHub lists (`changesPullRequestsOf` → `getChangesSince`'s fourth argument, re-read when GitHub's answer lands; `folderPullRequest` in main keeps those of the `origin` repository and matches the checked-out branch, else the head commit, else, for a detached worktree, a commit behind or past that head that is not already on its base). Without a match, a fetched base ref or a base past the start, the read falls back to the default branch, then the start. A worktree's dialog section names its own ref. A read past `MAX_CHANGES` draws `N+` on the chip, and its line counts cover only the files read.
- The changes chip and dialog also count the worktrees the thread's agents made (`use-run-work-folders.ts`). A worktree is the thread's only if all three hold: main lists it (`listWorktrees(cwd, startedAtMs)`: a linked worktree made after the thread started — birth of its `.git` link file — never the main checkout and never a geniro task worktree, the direct children of `worktreesRoot()`), and the run's own tool calls name it (`POST /v1/chats/:runId/named-paths`). Each is read as a work folder (`getChangesSince(…, true)`: from where its history meets the start, past what upstream already has, its commits and edits to tracked files only — a file it never added, such as an agent's log, is not listed; the thread's own folder still lists those). Found folders are only ever added; each lists in its own dialog section. Listings go through `worktree-list.ts` (see _Git_ in `apps/ui/src/main/AGENTS.md`).
- The changes chip opens the review screen (`changes-review-dialog.tsx`, a full-window `Dialog` over the chat), fed by the same `useChatChanges` reading, re-read past the freshness floor when it opens and whenever the window regains focus while it is open. It stays read-only toward the tree: it never stages, reverts or writes to git. Each file is a card (`review-file-card.tsx`) drawn from git's unified text by `review-diff-model.ts` (`parseFileDiff`, word marks shared with `UnifiedDiff` through `diff-word-spans.ts`, highlighting through `components/ui/highlight-lines.ts`); `UnifiedDiff` keeps its own callers unchanged. "Show N more lines" reads the worktree file through `readFileLines` (desktop only, absent on a paired device).
- Viewed is per device, in thread UI memory, as two flags per file: `review-viewed:<folder>\0<path>\0<hash of body>` and `review-viewed-any:<folder>\0<path>`; a set `-any` beside an unset body key reads "Changed since you viewed it". The hash is FNV-1a in the renderer, never `crypto.subtle` (the phone's page is not a secure context).
- Review comments live in the daemon (`review_comments`, `use-review-comments.ts` re-read on `review_comments_changed` and on reconnect), never in git. Drafts go to the agent only through Send review, as ONE message (`sendChatReview` / `sendTeamReview`) carrying `payload.review`, which the transcript draws as a review card (`review-message-card.tsx`, twin parser `review-payload.ts`). A send while the agent works waits for the turn to end (a chat: no open turn; a team: `followUpDelivery` no longer queues) and a `RUN_BUSY` retries with the same `clientMessageId`; both routes answer `RUN_BUSY` while the agent's (a team: a lead's) turn runs, so a review is never delivered mid-turn. A comment is placed by `anchorComment` (its quote near its stored line, else anywhere in the file's rows: the hunks and the lines read around them, in line order), comparing lines without the hidden characters the daemon strips from a stored quote (`withoutHiddenText`, twin of the daemon's `hidden-text.ts`). One it cannot place is read from the file on disk at its stored lines (`quoteMatches`) and is Outdated only when those differ too; the agent answers each through `answer_review_comment`.
